    "contentType": "article",
    "title": "Why 2018 is the vintage to buy in Stellenbosch.",
    "titleLanguage": "en",
    "standfirst": {
      "source": "negotiated",
      "text": "A cool, late-ripening year gave the Cape's Bordeaux blends the structure to age a decade — here's what to look for, and what to lay down.",
      "languageTag": "en"
    },
    "body": [
      {
        "source": "negotiated",
        "text": "The 2018 harvest came in three weeks late and nobody was pleased about it at the time. Cellars that had planned for a February pick were still waiting in March, watching sugars crawl and acids hold.",
        "languageTag": "en"
      },
      {
        "source": "negotiated",
        "text": "That slow finish is the whole story of the vintage. The tannins are fine rather than plush, the alcohols sit lower than the decade's average, and the wines were awkward for two years before they started to make sense.",
        "languageTag": "en"
      },
      {
        "source": "negotiated",
        "text": "They make sense now. What follows is what we could establish about the year, and what we could not.",
        "languageTag": "en"
      }
    ],
    "imageUrl": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1510812431401-41d2bd2722f3?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=80",
    "author": { "name": "Nomsa Dlamini", "tier": "professional", "role": "sommelier" },
